/*
Another term that I have used on multiple occasions in previous lectures is the standard library. The go standard library. So what is the standard library in go? What does the concept of standard library mean in go programming? The standard library refers to a comprehensive collection of packages, modules that are included with every go installation. These packages provide essential functionalities for building and executing Go programs across different platforms and environments. The standard library covers a wide range of tasks including input output operations, networking, text processing, concurrency, cryptography, and much, much more. The concept of a standard library in programming languages is to provide a set of pre-written, reusable code modules that address common programming tasks. This library is maintained and distributed with the language itself, ensuring consistency and reliability across different implementations and versions. To use functionalities from the standard library in go, you typically need to import specific packages using the import statement. For example, to use the FMT package for formatted input and output, you would import it like this. Import and in double quotes we state the name of the package which is fmt. Once imported, you can access functions and types provided by the FMT package in your code. For instance, Fmt.println is used to print formatted output to the console. In our programs, we need to explicitly import packages from the standard library to use their functionalities in our go programs. Even though the standard library is part of every go installation. Go enforces a modular approach where only explicitly imported packages are accessible within your code. This approach helps in managing dependencies, optimizing executable size, and avoiding namespace collisions. In conclusion, the standard library in go is a crucial component that provides foundational functionalities for developing Go applications. While it's included with every go installation, you must import specific packages to access their features in your code. This modular approach promotes clarity, efficiency, and maintainability in go programming practices. Understanding and effectively utilizing the standard library empowers developers to build robust and scalable applications using the rich set of tools and utilities provided by the go language.
*/